    "summary": "Mellow Other One opens into an expansive jam ending in Morning Dew",
    "blurb": "The second set flows beautifully, highlighted by a meditative Other One that blossoms into a soaring jam and finishes with a powerful Morning Dew. Jerry’s wah‑wah textures on Playing In The Band add extra color, while Bill’s drums are crisp and well defined. Strong performances of He’s Gone, Truckin’ and Mississippi Half-Step round out the show.",
    "review": "June 29 1973 at the Universal Amphitheatre captures a Dead that was deep in its exploratory groove. Set 2 opens with a relaxed Mississippi Half‑Step Uptown Toodeloo before moving into The Promised Land, then launches into a superb He’s Gone → Truckin’ → The Other One sequence. Jerry Garcia melts away the Truckin’ theme, leads an introspective, melodic Other One that expands into one of the era’s most epic jams and finally resolves in a soaring Morning Dew. Keith Godchaux adds tasteful Fender Rhodes textures to Playing In The Band while Donna Jean’s voice drifts trippy over the groove. The mix is especially clear on the drums – Bill Kreutzmann’s playing sits front‑and‑center, and Bob Weir’s verse work shines with Wes‑Montgomery‑style chord voicings. Although much of Set 1 is missing from this recording, the available material showcases a band locked in, offering a quintessential snapshot of the 1973 sound.",
